The [1mfully-aliased call[22m is the interesting one. All four pointers are the same array, so the operation is not a general fused multiply-add — every element becomes [38;5;10mx*x + x[0m in place. Element [38;5;10mi[0m is read before it's written within an iteration, so aliasing doesn't corrupt results, but the function's apparent purpose is gone. I translated it as a single-slice function named [38;5;10mfma_array_all_aliased[0m with a comment recording why, instead of writing a faithful-looking four-slice signature that Rust's borrow checker would reject anyway.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mSigned overflow[22m is undefined behavior in C but the compiled binary wraps, so [38;5;10mx*x+x[0m for large inputs must wrap too. I used [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m; a plain [38;5;10m*[0m would panic in debug and this is exactly the case the instructions say to reproduce, not correct.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m whitespace handling[22m — it skips arbitrary whitespace including newlines, so one integer can span line boundaries. I wrote a byte-at-a-time [38;5;10mScanner[0m with single-byte pushback modeling [38;5;10mungetc[0m, rather than a line-based reader, since line-based splitting would diverge on input like [38;5;10m1\n2   3[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mInteger parsing at the extremes[22m required matching glibc specifically: it collects digits, converts via [38;5;10mstrtol[0m (which [3msaturates[23m at the [38;5;10mlong[0m limits), then narrows to [38;5;10mint[0m. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m, not a parse error. Rust's [38;5;10mstr::parse::<i32>()[0m would reject it and break the read loop early, changing the output. This is implementation-defined territory, so it's matched to the glibc/x86-64 reference the C was compiled against — a different libc could differ here.[0m[0m
